# I²C Multi-Sensor Bus Design

## Overview
This project uses the I²C bus to interface the LPS22HB barometric pressure
sensor on the STM32 B-L475E-IOT01A Discovery board. The same I²C bus is shared
with other on-board sensors, demonstrating a realistic multi-device bus
topology.

---

## Bus Topology
- Bus type: I²C (two-wire)
- Master: STM32L475 MCU
- Slaves:
  - LPS22HB (Pressure sensor)
  - HTS221 (Temperature & Humidity sensor)
- Lines:
  - SCL: PB8
  - SDA: PB9

---

## Electrical Characteristics
- I²C lines are open-drain
- Pull-up resistors are provided on-board
- No external pull-ups are required

---

## Addressing
Each sensor on the bus has a unique 7-bit address, allowing multiple devices
to coexist on the same physical bus without conflict.

The BSP layer abstracts device addressing and transaction sequencing.

---

## Clock Speed
- Bus operates in Standard Mode (100 kHz)
- Suitable for low-bandwidth environmental sensing
- Reduces EMI and power consumption

---

## Arbitration and Reliability
- Only one master is present
- Clock stretching may occur internally
- HAL and BSP layers handle NACK and retry conditions

---

## Design Rationale
Using a shared I²C bus:
- Minimizes pin usage
- Enables sensor scalability
- Reflects real embedded system design constraints

This configuration mirrors production IoT and sensor-hub architectures.
